dosa 0004
  kala: 2026-08-20T09:52:23Z
  karta: cf-dosalekha
  jati: upadhi-anaviskrta
  vastu: exp27's running-law constant, as carried in notes/BLOCKS.md and notes/CROSSREVIEW_THMJ.md
  yatna: transport a measured leading coefficient into a stated law -- i.e. treat a fit over one decade of log Q as the constant it estimates
  hetu: no equivalence exists between a fitted coefficient and the constant it estimates, and the gap is not small here: the published range was 0.362 to 0.421 and the true value is exactly 1/4.  The two sides are different objects -- one is a property of a window, the other of the function -- so there is nothing to transport along
  hetu: the condition that defeats the generalisation (the upadhi) was available and was not sought.  A constant measured at one scale hides its scale dependence; HOLOGRAM.md section 7 records the same shape, where a "measured" noise floor of about 1e-3 was X^(-1/2) and deriving it moved a depth law from T log^2 T to T^(1/2) log^(3/2) T
  nasta: notes/BLOCKS.md:531 -- "($0.362$ by LSQ, $0.421$ by mean extraction, flagged method-sensitive by the"
  nasta: notes/CROSSREVIEW_THMJ.md:96 -- "running-law fit $0.3620\log^2Q + 0.1923\log Q + 0.3325$ (rms 0.1441), and"
  nasta: notes/CROSSREVIEW_THMJ.md:205 -- "vs exp27's $0.362/+0.19/+0.33$ (rms 0.144). Over one decade of $\log Q$,"
  nasta: notes/HISTORY_DIGEST.md:270 -- "that was published as $0.362$--$0.421$) are the worked demonstration."
  nasta: what the fit destroyed is the exact value and everything downstream of knowing it: 0.362 and 0.421 differ by 16 percent from each other and neither is 0.25.  The error propagated into two notes, a paper section, and a round of cross-review before being caught
  nasta: the two figures also destroyed their own disagreement's content -- "method-sensitive" was recorded as a caveat, when a method-sensitive constant is a proof that the object being measured is not the constant
  yogyata-drsta: grep over notes/ for the literal 0.362, four hits in three files, each read in place; CLAUDE.md's account of the incident; notes/HISTORY_DIGEST.md:270
  yogyata-ksetra: the notes/ directory of this repository.  Not the paper sources, not the Lean lane, not any external record of exp27
  yogyata-avadhi: NO claim that these particular lines are unmarked today -- HISTORY_DIGEST names it as a worked demonstration, i.e. the corpus already knows.  The record here is of the DEFECT and its witnesses, so that the count of this kind is not zero and can be compared later
  sesa: the operative test the corpus derived from it, handed forward: a correlation coefficient has no content, the content is the error term; if you cannot derive the error term you do not understand the object, and if you can, you do not need the run
  pramana: CLAUDE.md, "The rule" -- fitted coefficients over one decade are not results, they are noise with error bars omitted
  pramana: Nyaya on upadhi -- vyapti is killed by an upadhi, and the upadhi must be sought, not waited for (AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA section 19: उपाधिरन्वेष्टव्यः, न प्रतीक्ष्यः)
  punarabhinaya: grep -n '0\.362' notes/BLOCKS.md notes/CROSSREVIEW_THMJ.md notes/HISTORY_DIGEST.md
  phala: out~0.362
  sara: 3f7ecc8df79c1a0d
.
dosa 0005
  kala: 2026-08-20T09:52:58Z
  karta: cf-dosalekha
  jati: sankramana-asambhava
  vastu: collab/FAILURES.md, 1165 lines, 67 entries
  yatna: transport collab/FAILURES.md into this store -- read its 67 entries as defects and write them as records, so that the machine has ONE place a refusal lives
  hetu: no equivalence exists between the two.  A walk-yield says WHAT WAS LEARNED BY GOING; a dosa says WHAT WOULD HAVE BEEN DESTROYED BY COLLAPSING.  Those are not the same object under different names, so there is no e : A ~= B to transport along, and any mapping would be a choice imposed rather than an identification exhibited
  hetu: and the file says so itself.  It was reframed on 2026-08-12 by upstream directive away from the word failure: "every completed walk is information", and its norm is that no walk ends without emitting its YIELD.  A yield is positive content.  Forcing each of the 67 to name a loss it did not incur would manufacture 67 losses
  nasta: what transporting them here would destroy, exhibited from the file's own text: "Reading them as a sequence IS the program's actual trajectory: F6 produced Theorem D1, F8 produced Prop 2.1, F10 produced the corpus prior-art norm, F13 produced the E1-E7 gate map, F14 produced the frontier pivot.  The ledger is not a graveyard; it is the derivative of the program."  A dosa store has no field for "produced Theorem D1" and no ordering that is a trajectory
  nasta: the SEQUENCE itself.  This store is chained by sara in write order, which is a tamper order, not a research order.  F6-then-D1 is a causal reading of consecutive entries; sara-order carries no such reading and would silently offer one
  nasta: and in the other direction, what importing them would destroy HERE: every one of the 67 would fail validation for want of nasta, yogyata-drsta, yogyata-ksetra, yogyata-avadhi and punarabhinaya.  Passing them would mean deleting those requirements, i.e. destroying the only thing this store adds
  nasta: F1 as the concrete case: "Pair measure as complex-phased object (APPENDIX_D section D.6(3) first form). Died: sign error -- the measure is real with positive MS masses; corrected via the PRODUCT/string-kernel resolution."  That is a corrected error with a repair, which is neither a transport nor a written defect; it is a third thing the sutra's two roads do not classify, and it is fine as it stands
  yogyata-drsta: collab/FAILURES.md read from line 1 through its format statement and F1; the entry count taken as `grep -c '^F[0-9]' collab/FAILURES.md` = 67; the reframe note of 2026-08-12 in its header
  yogyata-ksetra: this one file.  The other append-only ledgers -- collab/orchestration/machine-ledger.tsv, collab/chronicle, the journals -- were not surveyed and no verdict is offered on them
  yogyata-avadhi: NO claim that FAILURES.md is deficient.  The verdict is only that it and this store are two standpoints on refusal and that neither reduces to the other.  If somebody later exhibits an equivalence, the transport becomes available and this record is superseded, not struck
  sesa: the standing question handed forward: a third object, neither transport nor defect, keeps appearing -- the CORRECTED error, where something wrong was made right.  GATE_AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md is a whole file of them.  The sutra says there is no third road; a corrected error is a defect written and then answered, which is why this store answers by APPENDING with uttara rather than by editing
  pramana: AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA section 6 -- यत्र संक्रमणं न सम्भवति तत्र दोषो लिख्यते
  pramana: AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA section 7 -- नयभेदे सङ्क्षेपो न विद्यते: where the standpoints differ, the collapse DOES NOT EXIST.  Not forbidden, not impolite -- does not exist.  एतद् नैतिकं वचनं न भवति । सांरचनिकम् ।
  pramana: collab/FAILURES.md header, reframe of 2026-08-12
  punarabhinaya: grep -c '^F[0-9]' collab/FAILURES.md
  phala: out~67
  sara: da33339322757b1f
.
dosa 0006
  kala: 2026-08-20T09:53:38Z
  karta: cf-dosalekha
  jati: avaktavya
  vastu: notes/D0020_LEDGER.md rows 5.5 and 5.11
  yatna: state, in one verdict line of one ledger, what this corpus's treatment of ABSENCE is -- reading the Vaisesika padartha row and the syadvada row as entries in a single technical register
  hetu: the two rows are two schools that reject each other's categories, and here they are being asserted TOGETHER, in one table, under one grading scheme.  Akalanka's distinction is exactly this: in succession (kramarpana) both are sayable; simultaneously (saharpana) no single utterance carries them.  This is the fourth bhanga, avaktavyam -- not unknown, not undefined, not empty
  hetu: so the defect is not that either row is wrong.  Both are careful.  The defect is that the register they share has no place to say that Jaina logicians reject the Naiyayika treatment of negation and Naiyayikas reject anekantavada, and a reader takes the table as one toolkit
  nasta: notes/D0020_LEDGER.md:310 -- "5.5 | वैशेषिक: the seven padarthas ... absence is not the empty word ... notes/ABHAVA.md section 1 gives absence three named slots -- pratiyogin (counterpositive), anuyogin (locus), avacchedaka (limitor) -- and writes abhava(p,l,a) = for all x in l: not p_a(x), i.e. absence is a SCOPED RELATION, not an entry"
  nasta: notes/D0020_LEDGER.md:316 -- "5.11 | syadvada's seven-valued scheme | CLASSICAL | saptabhangi.  Correctly counted at seven.  Not read; section 16"
  nasta: what one verdict destroys, named: the Nyaya-Vaisesika pratiyogin is a COUNTERPOSITIVE -- the absence is of a specific thing, in a specific locus, under a specific limitor, and that triple is the content.  The Jaina syad-nasti's ground is fourfold (dravya, ksetra, kala, bhava) and its nasti is a STANDPOINT-INDEXED denial, not a scoped relation.  Collapsing them keeps the part that converts to a formalism and discards which school is being spoken
  nasta: and it destroys the dispute, which is frequently the content: the count of lines in that 762-line file mentioning that these two schools reject each other is 0
  yogyata-drsta: notes/D0020_LEDGER.md, 762 lines, both rows read in full; the co-occurrence search over notes/*.md for pratiyogin together with syadvada or syad-nasti, which returns two files
  yogyata-ksetra: notes/*.md only.  Not formal/, not machine/, not collab/.  The hook .claude/hooks/source-coverage.sh check 3 already fires on WRITES with this shape; this record is about what is already on disk, which no hook has ever looked at
  yogyata-avadhi: NO verdict is offered on which school is right about absence, and none is possible from here -- that is the point of the entry.  Nor is this a claim that the ledger's author was careless: the register offered no field for the dispute
  sesa: handed forward: the fourth bhanga is POSITIVE and this store now has a kind for it, so an avaktavya can be recorded WITHOUT being resolved.  The next step is not to pick a school; it is to say, in that ledger, what the two schools would say to each other about the seventh slot
  pramana: Akalanka, Laghiyastraya, c. 720-780 -- kramarpana against saharpana, succession against simultaneity
  pramana: Siddhasena Divakara, Sanmatitarka; Umasvati, Tattvarthasutra 5.31 arpitanarpitasiddheh
  pramana: Kumarila and the Bhatta Mimamsakas on abhava/anupalabdhi, against Prabhakara who folds it into perception -- the dispute is live and this record takes no side
  pramana: CLAUDE.md, "Rival schools are not one toolkit"
  punarabhinaya: grep -c 'pratiyogin' notes/D0020_LEDGER.md; grep -c 'saptabhangi\|syadvada' notes/D0020_LEDGER.md; grep -ci 'Naiyayika' notes/D0020_LEDGER.md
  phala: out~1
  sara: 3d2096864e963ff4
.
dosa 0007
  kala: 2026-08-20T09:54:27Z
  karta: cf-dosalekha
  jati: ayogya-darsana
  vastu: machine/dosa.lekha dosa 0006, field phala
  yatna: record, in dosa 0006, the outcome its replay would produce -- three counts over notes/D0020_LEDGER.md
  hetu: I wrote `phala: out~1` without running the command.  The counts are 2, 0, 0.  The looking that produced the expected value was not a looking at all; it was a guess with the shape of a measurement, which is the exact failure CLAUDE.md's oldest rule names -- a number asserted where a derivation or a run was available and cheaper
  hetu: and a second unfitness, in the command itself: `grep -c 'saptabhangi\|syadvada'` returns 0 on a file whose text is 'syādvāda' and 'saptabhaṅgī'.  An ASCII-folded search over Sanskrit terms is not fit to find them.  The 0 in that output is not evidence of absence; it is evidence of the wrong query
  nasta: the run, exhibited: `grep -c 'pratiyogin' notes/D0020_LEDGER.md` = 2 ; `grep -c 'saptabhangi\|syadvada' ...` = 0 ; `grep -ci 'Naiyayika' ...` = 0 -- against the recorded `out~1`
  nasta: what a silent edit of dosa 0006 would have destroyed: the fact that its writer asserted an unmeasured number, five minutes after writing dosa 0004 ABOUT asserting unmeasured numbers.  That is the whole content of this entry and it exists only because the record cannot be changed
  nasta: and what the folded grep destroyed: the true count for saptabhaṅgī in that file, which the diacritic-correct query finds.  A 0 that means 'not asked properly' and a 0 that means 'not there' are different objects, and the output line is the same character
  yogyata-drsta: the actual run of dosa 0006's punarabhinaya, output 2/0/0, exit 1; and the two spellings side by side in notes/D0020_LEDGER.md:310 and :316
  yogyata-ksetra: one record and one file.  No survey of the other records' phala values is claimed -- and every one of dosa 0001, 0002, 0003, 0004, 0005 WAS run before its phala was fixed, which is why only this one diverged
  yogyata-avadhi: this issues NO verdict on dosa 0006's content.  Its naṣṭa stand as written and were read out of the file directly.  Only the expected replay OUTCOME was unwarranted, and only that is corrected here
  sesa: the corrected replay, handed forward: grep -c 'pratiyogin' notes/D0020_LEDGER.md gives 2, and the dispute-mention count that matters stays 0.  The substantive finding of dosa 0006 is unchanged: 0 lines of 762 name that the two schools reject each other
  sesa: and the general rule: RUN the punarabhinaya before recording its phala.  A phala written from expectation is a fitted constant with one data point and no error bar
  pramana: Kumarila Bhatta, Slokavarttika, Abhavapariccheda, c. 7th c. -- yogya-anupalabdhi; and AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA section 19, "यत्र दृश्येत तत्र न दृष्टम्" इति प्रमाणम्
  pramana: CLAUDE.md, "The rule": before running any computation write down the theorem it would replace; and fitted coefficients are noise with error bars omitted
  uttara: 0006
  punarabhinaya: grep -c 'pratiyogin' notes/D0020_LEDGER.md; grep -ci 'Naiyayika' notes/D0020_LEDGER.md
  phala: out~2
  sara: 5f0522f3eab4a2ee
